Step 1 — Structured Response Requirements
The AI response must contain an alerts array.
Each alert must contain exactly four fields:
* icon
* title
* description
* priority
Response Structure
{
  "alerts": [
    {
      "icon": "string",
      "title": "string",
      "description": "string",
      "priority": "low | medium | high"
    }
  ]
}
Field Requirements
1. icon
The icon must be one of the following exact values:
* abs
* airbag
* battery
* check_engine
* esp
* gas_tank
* tire_pressure
* washer_fluid
* oil
* coolant_temperature
* spark_plug
* warning
Use warning for an issue that does not correspond to one of the specific icons above.
Never create or return an icon that is not on this list.
2. title
* Must describe the vehicle issue.
* Must be 20 characters or fewer.
* Keep it short and easy for the driver to understand.
3. description
* Must briefly explain the issue.
* Must be 50 characters or fewer.
* Keep the description simple and understandable to the average driver.
4. priority
Priority must be exactly one of:
* low
* medium
* high
The application will determine the icon color based on the priority. The AI should not return a color.
Multiple Issues
If multiple issues are detected, return multiple objects in the alerts array.
Example:
{
  "alerts": [
    {
      "icon": "check_engine",
      "title": "Check Engine Light",
      "description": "Engine fault detected",
      "priority": "high"
    },
    {
      "icon": "tire_pressure",
      "title": "Low Tire Pressure",
      "description": "One or more tires may be low",
      "priority": "medium"
    }
  ]
}
No Issues
If no recognizable vehicle issues are present, return an empty array:
{
  "alerts": []
}
Do not create a “No Issues” alert. The application will interpret an empty alerts array as no issues detected.
That’s the complete Step 1 specification. You can use this as the blueprint when we do Step 2 and build the actual Structured Outputs schema.
